<title>COLOMBIA: Uganda sees coffee output up 50 percent by 2000.</title>
<headline>Uganda sees coffee output up 50 percent by 2000.</headline>
<dateline>CARTAGENA, Colombia 1996-11-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Uganda, which produced about 4.0 million 60-kg bags of coffee in the crop year ending last September, expects to increase its output by as much as 50 percent by the turn of the century, a top industry official said Friday.</p>
<p>Paul Mugambwa, president of the Uganda Coffee Trade Federation, said he sees Uganda producing 5.5 to as much as 6.0 million bags by 2000.</p>
<p>&quot;The industry is growing, it's very healthy,&quot; Mugambwa told Reuters.</p>
<p>Coffee is the major traditional cash crop exported from Uganda and robusta accounts for about 90 percent of its production.</p>
<p>Mugambwa was in Cartagena to address a three-day annual meeting of the National Coffee Exporters' Association.</p>
<p>-- Cartagena newsroom, 525 665 0666, extension 1046</p>
